1. Docker Fundamentals
1.1 What is Docker?
Docker is a platform for developing, packaging, and running applications in containers. A container packages an
application together with the libraries and configuration it needs, helping it run consistently across environments.
1.2 Why Docker is used for deployment
• Consistency: the same packaged application can be tested and deployed in different environments.
• Portability: a Docker image can run wherever a compatible Docker runtime is available.
• Fast deployment: containers generally start much faster than full virtual machines.
• Isolation: applications can run in separate containers with controlled resources and networking.
• Reproducibility: Dockerfiles can describe how an image is built.
• Scalability: containers fit well with microservices and orchestrators.
1.3 The 'works on my machine' problem
An application may work on a developer's laptop but fail on another machine because of different operating-system
libraries, runtime versions, dependencies, environment variables, or configuration. Docker reduces this mismatch by
packaging the application and its required environment into an image.
Simple idea: Code + Dependencies + Configuration → Docker Image → Container → Running Application
1.4 Important Docker terms
Term Meaning
Image Read-only template used to create containers.
Container A running or stopped instance created from an image.
Dockerfile Text file containing instructions for building an image.
Registry Repository/service used to store and distribute images.
Docker Hub A public/private container image registry service.
Volume Docker-managed storage used to persist data.
Network Mechanism that allows containers to communicate.
Compose Tool for defining and running multi-container applications.
2. Docker Architecture
At a high level, Docker uses a client-server architecture. The Docker CLI sends commands to the Docker daemon,
which manages images, containers, networks, and volumes.
Component Role
Docker CLI / Client Accepts commands such as docker run and docker build.
Docker Daemon Background service that manages Docker objects and operations.
Docker Engine Core Docker technology used to build and run containers.
Image Packaged application template.
Container Runtime instance of an image.
Docker Deployment Notes — DevOps Assignment Page 3
Component Role
Registry Stores and distributes images.
Network Connects containers and external clients.
Volume Provides persistent storage.
Flow: User → Docker CLI → Docker Daemon/Engine → Image/Container/Network/Volume
3. Installation & First Container
3.1 Installation
Install Docker Desktop on Windows/macOS, or install Docker Engine using the official instructions for a Linux
distribution. After installation, verify that the Docker CLI can communicate with the engine.
docker --version
docker info
3.2 Download an image
docker pull nginx
The command downloads the Nginx image from a configured registry. If no registry is specified, Docker commonly
uses Docker Hub.
3.3 Run the first container
docker run nginx
docker run -d --name my-nginx nginx
docker ps
docker ps -a
-d runs the container in detached/background mode. --name assigns a readable container name.
4. Docker Images
An image is an immutable package made of filesystem layers and metadata. Images are normally identified using a
repository name and optional tag, such as nginx:latest or my-app:1.0.
docker images
docker image ls
docker pull nginx:latest
docker image inspect nginx
docker image rm nginx:latest
4.1 Image vs Container
Image Container
Template/package Runtime instance
Usually immutable Has a writable container layer
Stored locally or in a registry Created and managed by Docker
Used to create containers Runs the application
One image can be used to create many containers.
5. Container Lifecycle & Management
A common lifecycle is: create → start → running → stop → start again → remove.
Docker Deployment Notes — DevOps Assignment Page 4
docker create --name web nginx
docker start web
docker stop web
docker restart web
docker rm web
docker ps
docker ps -a
5.1 Logs and shell access
docker logs my-nginx
docker logs -f my-nginx
docker exec -it my-nginx sh
docker logs helps inspect application output. docker exec runs a command inside an already-running container.
6. Dockerfile & Image Building
A Dockerfile is a text file containing ordered instructions used to build an image. Each instruction contributes to the
resulting image and its metadata.
6.1 Example Dockerfile
FROM node:20
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install
COPY . .
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["npm", "start"]
6.2 Meaning of the instructions
Instruction Purpose
FROM Selects the base image.
WORKDIR Sets the working directory.
COPY Copies files from build context into the image.
RUN Executes a command while building the image.
EXPOSE Documents the port the application listens on.
CMD Defines the default command when a container starts.
6.3 Build an image
docker build -t my-app:1.0 .
docker images
The final dot (.) tells Docker to use the current directory as the build context. The tag my-app:1.0 gives the image a
repository/name and version.
7. Application Deployment with Docker
A basic deployment packages an application into an image, then starts a container from that image and publishes
the required application port.
7.1 Run a custom application
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 --name my-app my-app:1.0
Port mapping: host port : container port. Here, requests arriving at host port 3000 are forwarded to port 3000 in the
container.
Docker Deployment Notes — DevOps Assignment Page 5
Browser → Host:3000 → Docker port mapping → Container:3000 → Application
7.2 Verify deployment
docker ps
docker logs my-app
docker logs -f my-app
docker exec -it my-app sh
7.3 Stop/remove
docker stop my-app
docker rm my-app
8. Docker Volumes & Persistent Storage
Containers are disposable. Data written only to a container's writable layer can disappear when the container is
removed. Volumes provide a Docker-managed way to persist data independently of a particular container.
docker volume ls
docker volume create app-data
docker volume inspect app-data
docker volume rm app-data
8.1 Mount a volume
docker run -d -v app-data:/data --name data-container IMAGE
The volume app-data is mounted at /data inside the container. The exact path depends on the application.
Use volumes for: databases, uploaded files, application state, and other data that must survive container replacement.
9. Docker Networking
Docker networking lets containers communicate with one another and with external systems. User-defined networks
are commonly used to connect application components.
docker network ls
docker network create app-network
docker network inspect app-network
docker network rm app-network
9.1 Connect a container to a network
docker run -d --name backend --network app-network backend-image
docker run -d --name frontend --network app-network frontend-image
On a user-defined network, containers can communicate using container/service names and the appropriate ports.
Avoid exposing internal-only services to the host unless needed.
10. Docker Compose
Docker Compose is used to define and run applications made of multiple services. A Compose file can describe
services, networks, volumes, images/build instructions, environment variables, and ports.
10.1 Typical architecture
