13. Docker in CI/CD
Docker fits into CI/CD by making the application build artifact a container image. A pipeline can automatically build,
test, scan, publish, and deploy that image.
Example: GitHub → CI runner → Docker build → tests/scan → registry → deployment server → container
• Jenkins can build and deploy Docker images.
• GitHub Actions can automate Docker build/push workflows.
• GitLab CI/CD can build, test, publish, and deploy containers.
• Azure DevOps can automate container image pipelines and deployments.
Important: CI/CD tools automate the process; Docker provides the packaging/runtime mechanism.
14. Docker Security & Best Practices
• Use trusted and maintained base images.
• Pin versions/tags when reproducibility matters instead of relying blindly on floating tags.
• Keep images and dependencies updated.
• Run containers with the least privilege necessary; avoid unnecessary root privileges.
• Do not hard-code passwords, API keys, or tokens in Dockerfiles or images.
• Use secrets/environment configuration appropriately.
• Expose only required ports.
• Scan images for known vulnerabilities.
• Use a small, appropriate base image where practical.
• Add a .dockerignore file to keep unnecessary files out of the build context.
14.1 Example .dockerignore
