17. Key Advantages & Limitations
Advantages
• Fast and repeatable application deployment.
• Consistent application environments.
• Portable packaging.
• Efficient resource use compared with full virtual machines in many workloads.
• Easy integration with CI/CD.
• Supports microservices and isolated application components.
Limitations / Challenges
• Container security still requires careful configuration.
• Persistent storage and networking require planning.
• Large-scale deployments may require an orchestrator such as Kubernetes.
• Not every workload benefits equally from containers.
18. Docker vs Virtual Machines
Aspect Docker Containers Virtual Machines
Virtualization OS-level isolation Hardware virtualization
Typical size Usually smaller Usually larger
Startup Usually fast Usually slower
OS Share host kernel model Each VM has its own guest OS
Use Apps, services, CI/CD Full OS isolation and legacy workloads
Containers and VMs are not direct replacements in every situation. They solve related but different
deployment/isolation needs.
